1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,095
After your product has been built and before you launch it,

2
00:00:04,095 --> 00:00:06,600
you'll need to test it to make sure that it's working

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,360
correctly and meeting user expectations.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,940
This can happen through QA testing,

5
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:15,915
but it's also important to get feedback from real life usage.

6
00:00:15,915 --> 00:00:20,250
Some issues won't be discovered from just QA testing alone.

7
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:22,560
Once you've identified issues,

8
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:26,100
you'll also have to work through how to prioritize them so

9
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:30,465
that the team is focused on fixing the most impactful issues first.

10
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:33,485
Finally, before you can launch,

11
00:00:33,485 --> 00:00:39,065
you should run a go/no-go meeting to clearly articulate the status of your project,

12
00:00:39,065 --> 00:00:42,865
risks and mitigations to your stakeholders.

13
00:00:42,865 --> 00:00:46,370
All of these activities are focused around

14
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:49,610
shipping a high-quality product and making sure

15
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:52,260
that all stakeholders are informed and on

16
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:55,895
the same page when it comes to lunch status and open issues.

17
00:00:55,895 --> 00:01:00,790
When it comes to test plans we'll cover what is a test plan?

18
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:03,695
What are functional requirements?

19
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:06,350
What are non-functional requirements and

20
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:09,185
how are they different from functional requirements?

21
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:11,510
How to work with the QA team,

22
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:14,450
and what happens when issues are discovered.

23
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:17,485
Then we'll talk about dogfood programs.

24
00:01:17,485 --> 00:01:23,990
Specifically, what is dogfooding and how does it differ from user testing?

25
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:26,584
Why is dogfooding valuable?

26
00:01:26,584 --> 00:01:29,525
How you can create a dogfood program.

27
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:34,325
How to get feedback from your dogfooders and once you have feedback,

28
00:01:34,325 --> 00:01:35,960
how to act on it.

29
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:39,590
As you start to identify issues from testing and dogfooding,

30
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:41,465
you'll need to prioritize them.

31
00:01:41,465 --> 00:01:44,975
We'll talk about why prioritization is important.

32
00:01:44,975 --> 00:01:48,310
What are different types of issues you might encounter,

33
00:01:48,310 --> 00:01:53,965
and how you can use a prioritization framework to guide your decision-making process.

34
00:01:53,965 --> 00:01:56,585
In the last part of this lesson,

35
00:01:56,585 --> 00:02:00,605
we'll cover how to run a go/no-go launch meeting.

36
00:02:00,605 --> 00:02:05,575
We'll start by learning what exactly is a go no-go meeting,

37
00:02:05,575 --> 00:02:08,825
how you can run a go no-go meeting,

38
00:02:08,825 --> 00:02:14,970
and how to handle a meeting when the answer is no. Let's get started.

